/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 154 0 R
/E (For MBA \(Rural Management\) students, NEP presents opportunities that extend beyond the classroom and fieldwork based experimental learning. Rural managers after jumping into the ocean of development can contribute by collaborating with schools, Panchayati Raj Institutions, civil society organizations, and local communities to improve educational access and quality. They can facilitate community participation in school governance, strengthen School Management Committees, promote digital literacy, encourage girls' education, support vocational training initiatives, and mobilize resources for educational development. Their expertise in project management, social mobilization, monitoring, and stakeholder engagement can significantly enhance the implementation of educational initiatives in rural areas.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
296 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 154 0 R
/E (The success of NEP ultimately depends on effective implementation and collective action. Governments, educational institutions, civil society organizations, private sector partners, and local communities must work together to ensure that policy aspirations translate into meaningful learning outcomes. Investments in teacher development, school infrastructure, digital inclusion, community engagement, and continuous monitoring will determine whether rural education can truly become a driver of inclusive and sustainable development.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
297 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 154 0 R
/E (As future rural development professionals, MBA \(Rural Management\) students have a vital role in shaping this transformation. Education is not only about improving literacy rates; it is about building human capabilities, empowering communities, reducing inequalities, and creating opportunities for sustainable livelihoods. The National Education Policy provides a comprehensive roadmap towards this vision. By embracing its principles and actively contributing to their implementation, rural managers can help ensure that every village becomes a centre of learning, innovation, and inclusive growth.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
298 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 154 0 R
/E (An important contribution of NEP is its emphasis on vocational education and skill development. The policy envisions integrating vocational learning into mainstream education from the middle school level onwards. Students are encouraged to gain practical exposure through internships, local crafts, agriculture, entrepreneurship, and community-based occupations. Such an approach is particularly meaningful in rural areas, where education can be directly linked to local livelihoods, sustainable agriculture, rural enterprises, and emerging opportunities in the green economy. By connecting education with employability, NEP seeks to prepare rural youth not only for higher education but also for productive and dignified livelihoods. The policy further advocates experiential learning, critical thinking, problem-solving, and multidisciplinary education. These pedagogical approaches resonate strongly with the philosophy of Rural Management, where understanding community realities, participatory development, and field-based learning are central to professional practice. Students trained under such an educational framework are better equipped to address complex rural development challenges through innovation, collaboration, and evidence-based decision-making.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
