2062 0 obj
<<
/Title (Education has long been recognized as the foundation of rural development. It equips individuals with knowledge, skills, and values that enable them to improve their livelihoods, participate in democratic processes, and contribute to the overall development of the communities. In India, where nearly two-thirds of the population resides in rural areas, strengthening rural education is not merely an educational priority but a developmental imperative. The National Education Policy \(NEP\) marks a significant shift in the country's educational vision by emphasizing equity, quality, inclusivity, effectiveness, transparency, sustainability and lifelong learning. For students of Rural Management, the policy offers valuable insights into how education can become a transformative force for sustainable rural development. Recognizing these realities, NEP introduces a holistic and learner-centric approach to education. One of its most significant reforms is the restructuring of the school education system into the 5+3+3+4 curricular agendas, replacing the conventional 10+2 model. This structure aligns learning with children's cognitive development and emphasizes early childhood care and education as the foundation for lifelong learning. For rural children, this reform has the potential to reduce learning gaps from the earliest stages of schooling. The other key feature of NEP is its strong emphasis on foundational literacy and numeracy. Through initiatives such as the National Mission on Foundational Literacy and Numeracy i.e. NIPUN Bharat, the policy seeks to ensure that every child acquires essential reading and mathematical skills by grade 3. This focus is particularly relevant for rural schools, where first-generation learners often require additional academic support to remain engaged in education. The policy also promotes multilingual education by encouraging the use of the mother tongue or regional language as the medium of instruction in the past decades. Research consistently demonstrates that children learn concepts more effectively when taught in their local dialect. In multilingual rural India, this approach not only enhances comprehension but also preserves local cultures, traditions, and indigenous knowledge systems. By integrating local contexts into classroom learning, the policy strengthens the connection between education and community life. Technology has emerged as another transformative pillar of the policy. Digital learning platforms, online teacher training, virtual laboratories, and educational resources have the potential to improve the quality of education in remote villages.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2057 0 R
>>
endobj
